Non-specific Lower Back Pain
Common Infections and Afflictions
Dull lumbosacral ache, Paraspinal muscle spasm, Normal neurological examination

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: CIA-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower back pain rendering the patient immobile.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified an adult patient experiencing a sudden, debilitating exacerbation of lower back pain following a minor lifting movement, resulting in the patient being completely immobilized on the floor due to severe muscle spasm. The acute inability to ambulate or transfer independently raises significant clinical concern. Urgent home assessment is clinically mandated to perform a rigorous neurological examination to definitively rule out cauda equina syndrome or acute spinal cord compression. Delaying this assessment poses an unacceptable risk of permanent neurological deficit if a surgical emergency is missed, strictly satisfying the criteria for an urgent after-hours MBS assessment (MBS 585-600).

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: I bent over to pick up a dropped pen, and my lower back completely seized up; I haven't been able to get off the floor for two hours.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms initiated 2 hours ago following a minor mechanical action (bending). The patient reports an intense, dull, and aching pain localized specifically to the lumbosacral region. The pain is severely exacerbated by any attempted movement or lifting of the torso, causing massive, visible localized paraspinal muscle spasm. Relief of pain is strictly achieved only with complete recumbency. The patient explicitly denies any radiation of pain below the knee, numbness, "pins and needles," or weakness in the legs. Crucially, the patient denies any bowel or bladder dysfunction (incontinence or retention). Lack of red flag symptoms such as night sweats, unexplained weight loss, or major trauma.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound/floor-bound; completely unable to ambulate or transfer independently.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone. High acute distress due to severe mechanical immobility.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 96 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Alert, intensely uncomfortable, lying strictly supine on the floor. Visibly grimacing upon any attempt to shift position.
Recent/Relevant Labs: None available. Normal plain lumbosacral radiographs in the absence of red flags would assist in confirming the diagnosis chronologically, but are strictly contraindicated in the acute phase as they offer zero diagnostic value for soft tissue spasm.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the lumbosacral spine reveals massive, board-like localized paraspinal muscle spasm and extreme tenderness to palpation bilaterally. There is no palpable step-off or midline bony tenderness over the spinous processes. Lower extremity neurological examination is entirely normal: reflexes (patellar, Achilles) are 2+ and symmetrical, strength is 5/5 bilaterally in all myotomes, and sensation is perfectly intact to light touch. Straight leg raise test is definitively negative bilaterally (no radicular pain reproduced down the leg). Anal sphincter tone is normal (if clinically indicated based on history, though history of continence was explicitly confirmed).
Environmental Observations: Safe residence, but patient requires assistance to transfer to a bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Patient has been unable to reach their medication cabinet to self-administer analgesia.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Non-specific Lower Back Pain - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:
Cauda Equina Syndrome - Definitively ruled out by the explicitly normal bowel/bladder function, normal perianal sensation, and entirely intact lower limb neurology (strength, reflexes, sensation).
Herniated Nucleus Pulposus (Sciatica) - Ruled out clinically by the negative straight leg raise test and the absolute lack of radicular pain radiating below the knee in a specific dermatomal pattern.
Spinal Compression Fracture - Unlikely given the minor mechanical onset (bending for a pen rather than a heavy fall or lifting a massive weight) and the absence of midline bony point tenderness.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The sudden, paralyzing onset of lower back pain raised immediate, critical red flags for potential acute spinal cord compression or cauda equina syndrome, particularly given the patient's physical immobility. An urgent home assessment was essential to perform a thorough, targeted neurological examination. By confirming entirely intact lower limb neurology and negative radicular signs, a neurosurgical emergency was definitively excluded. The diagnosis of acute, benign mechanical muscle spasm allowed for the immediate administration of potent analgesics and physical assistance with transferring, averting a highly distressing and unnecessary ambulance call-out for a non-surgical issue. This assessment fulfills MBS 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Administered Ibuprofen 400mg and Paracetamol 1000mg orally stat. Prescribed a short course of a skeletal muscle relaxant (e.g., Diazepam 5mg up to twice daily for max 3 days) strictly to break the acute, paralyzing muscle spasm. Provided physical assistance (using safe manual handling techniques) to transfer the patient from the floor to a firm bed.
Positive Behavioral Change: To prevent your back muscles from fusing into a solid, painful block, you must begin gentle, short walks (even just 5 minutes around the house) every 2 hours, starting tomorrow morning. Strict, prolonged bed rest actually weakens the supporting muscles and chemically prolongs the intense spasm; early, gentle movement physically flushes the inflammatory chemicals out of the tissue.
Home-Based Services: None required.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Review with regular General Practitioner or Physiotherapist in 3-5 days to commence a structured core strengthening and mobility program.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 immediately if you suddenly lose control of your bowel or bladder, if you become unable to urinate despite feeling full, if your legs become progressively weak or give way, or if you develop numbness around your groin or buttocks.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical; x-rays are not indicated for non-specific back pain without red flags as they do not show the muscles or nerves.
Clinical Course: The acute, paralyzing spasm will peak over the next 48 hours and gradually soften with the muscle relaxants and gentle movement. The dull ache may persist for 2 to 4 weeks, but functional mobility will return rapidly.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the pain suddenly shoots violently down your leg past your knee, or if you develop a new, unexplained fever.

PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: Your back essentially experienced a 'cramp' of the massive stabilizing muscles, pulling them into a tight, protective spasm that has locked your spine. The muscle relaxants act to physically un-clench those muscles, allowing you to move, while the ibuprofen reduces the internal swelling.
Actionable Steps: Do not lift anything heavier than a cup of tea for the next 72 hours. Apply a heat pack to the lower back for 20 minutes every 2 hours to increase blood flow and physically relax the knotted muscle fibers.

